Manage Logs
.SH SYNOPSIS

[1] \fBlog\fR [\fBtype\fR [(\fBweb\fR | \fBrweb\fR | \fBfirewall\fR | \fBguard\fR | \fBantivirus\fR | \fBavserver\fR | \fBwaf\fR) [(\fBon\fR | \fBoff\fR) [(\fBon\fR | \fBoff\fR)]]]]

[2] \fBlog\fR [\fBrotate\fR [\fBreport\fR] | \fBforce\fR [\fBwait\fR]]

[3] \fBlog\fR [\fBsave\fR (\fBweb\fR | \fBrweb\fR | \fBfirewall\fR | \fBguard\fR | \fBantivirus\fR | \fBavserver\fR | \fBwaf\fR | \fBsystem\fR) \fI<serial>\fR (\fBftp\fR | \fBsftp\fR | \fBtftp\fR) \fI<file-server>\fR \fI<file-path>\fR]

[4] \fBlog\fR \fBsyslog\fR [\fBraz\fR | (\fBadd\fR | \fBdel\fR) (\fBudp\fR | \fBtcp\fR | \fBtls\fR) \fI<syslog-server>\fR [\fI<port>\fR] | \fBtest\fR]

[5] \fBlog\fR \fBsyslog\fR [\fBca\fR [\fBraz\fR | \fBset\fR \fI<ca-id>\fR]]

.SH DESCRIPTION

Log reports gives you visibility into all traffic and key events happening in the system. To benefit from the logging the logging mode should be activated (see the command \fBmode\fR). Eight types of logs are generated by the appliance:

\(bu \fBweb\fR log: reports traffic managed by the forwarding and transparent proxy.

\(bu \fBrweb\fR log: reports traffic managed by the reverse proxy.

\(bu \fBguard\fR log: reports attempts to access non-authorized Web sites.

\(bu \fBantivirus\fR log: reports attempts to access virus infected objects coming from the Web.

\(bu \fBavserver\fR log: reports attempts to access virus infected objects coming from the external systems such as an MTA (Mail Transfer Agent).

\(bu \fBwaf\fR log: reports unauthorized requests blocked by the Web Application Firewall.

\(bu \fBfirewall\fR log: reports denied packets by the IP firewall.

\(bu \fBsystem\fR log: reports low level system events (only for maintenance purpose).

Please note that an attempt to inject a virus on a Web server protected by the appliance (in reverse mode) is logged in the WAF log file and not in the antivirus log file (which is reserved for forwarding and transparent Web accesses).

All log types (except the \fBsystem\fR log) can be activated or deactivated. The first [1] usage form allows you to activated or deactivated log types. To activate a log type use the keyword \fBtype\fR followed by the required log type specifier (\fBweb\fR, \fBrweb\fR, \fBguard\fR, \fBantivirus\fR, \fBavserver\fR, \fBwaf\fR or \fBfirewall\fR) and the keyword \fBon\fR. To deactivate a log type use the keyword \fBoff\fR instead. When a log type is activated, you can optionality activate of deactivate the remote logging for it (see the fourth usage form below). The final argument in this usage form allows you to activate (\fBon\fR) or deactivate (\fBoff\fR) the remote logging (on syslog servers).

Without any arguments, this command displays a history of generated logs. Logs can be saved after their rotation and never when they are in use. To inspect live logs you can use the Web Auditing module (see the command \fBadmin\fR).

The system keeps logs of \fIn\fR days of activity archived on separate files for each day. The number \fIn\fR is called the retention period and is set up during the appliance installation. Log rotation is normally an automatic daily operation. However it is always possible to force a log rotation by using the command \fBlog rotate\fR. Use this if you want to download today's logs immediately without waiting for a daily log rotation. The log rotation operation runs in background unless the \fBforce wait\fR keywords are used. Without the optional argument \fBforce\fR the user is invited to confirm the log rotation operation. The optional \fBreport\fR keyword allows you to display a report of the last manual log rotation.

\fBImportant notice\fR: according to the number of users set up during the installation, an upper  limit is fixed for log sizes and the required storage space is reserved for them. If a log report grows abnormally too fast a log rotation is forced without waiting for the daily log rotation. This prevents the system from being saturated. In the case of an advanced rotation an SNMP trap is sent to configure SNMP receivers.

A log rotation is an asynchronous operation (you are not blocked during its execution). Note that log rotation may fail if an \fBapply\fR operation or another asynchronous operation is running.

Accesses to non-authorized objects are logged in separate files while authorized forwarding and reverse Web accesses are saved each in a distinct file. A \fBweb\fR or \fBrweb\fR log has the following format:

\fIclient-ip authuser [date] "request" status bytes cache-status cache-peer-status\fR where:

  \(bu \fIclient-ip\fR: the remote client IP address.

  \(bu \fIauthuser\fR: the user name by which the user has authenticated himself (if the authentication mode is activated).

  \(bu \fI[date]\fR: date and time of the request in RFC3339 format (with the caveat that minutes and hours in the time offset are not separated by a colon).

  \(bu \fI"request"\fR: the request line exactly as it came from the client.

  \(bu \fIstatus\fR: the HTTP status code returned to the client.

  \(bu \fIbytes\fR: the content-length of the object transferred (including headers).

  \(bu \fIcache-status\fR: the cache status (HIT, MISS...).

  \(bu \fIcache-peer-status\fR: peer cache status (HIT, MISS...).

  \(bu \fIUser Agent>\fR: the Web browser type used by the user.

Please note that the two last information are not present in a \fBrweb\fR log.

The second [2] usage form allows you to save logs on a file server. The argument \fI<serial>\fR specifies the serial number of the saved log. The most recent log has the number 1. The older one has the number 2 and so on. If you want to save logs on a remote file server for archiving purpose, your backup cycle must be equal to or less than the logs retention period described below.

Logs can be saved on a trusted remote file server. Trusted file servers are defined with the command \fBaccess\fR. All logs are saved in gzip compressed files. The full file name including the extension \fI.gz\fR should be specified. A system log is saved in gzip tar (archive) format. The third [3] usage form allows you to save logs.

In addition to be locally saved, logs can be sent in real time to remote syslog servers. The fourth [4] usage form allows you to manage remote syslog servers. To add a remote syslog server use the keywords \fBsyslog add\fR followed by a supported protocol, its server IP address (or DNS name) and its listening port. To delete a remote syslog server use the \fBdel\fR keyword instead of the \fBadd\fR keyword. To erase the list of all syslog servers use the keywords \fBsyslog raz\fR. The supported protocols are as follows:

  \(bu \fBtcp\fR: TCP (Transmission Control Protocol).

  \(bu \fBudp\fR: UDP (User Datagram Protocol).

  \(bu \fBtls\fR: TCP over TLS

To check connectivity with syslog servers, you can send test syslog messages with the \fIlocal7.info\fR priority to all configured servers by using the \fBtest\fR keyword. Please note that, as with any other command, the new configuration must be applied using the \fBapply\fR command before test syslog messages can be sent.

The SSL certificate presented by a remote syslog server using an SSL-based protocol is verified against the CA certificate that was used to sign it. The fifth [5] usage form allows you to specify the CA certificate ID (\fI<ca-id>\fR) to be used for verification. If no CA certificate is specified, the system's CA certificate is used. Please note that if a suitable CA certificate is not specified, logs will not be sent to the syslog servers. To add and import a CA certificate, see the \fBtls\fR command.

